1 NAME Natronai ben Hakhinai, wazir to Yūsuf bin ʿAbd al- Raḥmān bin Abī ʿUbayda al- Fihrī,
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 ADDR
3 CITY Narbonne
3 STAE Languedoc-Roussillon
3 CTRY France
1 BIRT
2 DATE 720
2 PLAC Pumbedita
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Babylon
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Natronai was essential to the success of Charles Martel at the Battle of Poitiers.  The Caliph of al-Andalus, an Arab named Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman, assisted Charles Martel in the battle against Berbers.  This affo
2 CONC rded great freedoms for Jews aligned with Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman.  After the Battle of Poitiers, Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman was appointed governor of Narbonne according to the Chronicle of Moissac, where he was in command of militar
2 CONC y operations. During four years he is said to have raided and pillaged the Lower Rhone, and in 735 he took Arles.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Yehudah ben Barzilai (Sefer ha’Ittim), “it was Natronai ben Hakhinai who wrote down the Talmud from memory” using no text as a “master” copy.  To clarify, Natroani ben Hakhina, according to Yehudah ben Barzilai recall
2 CONC ed the entire Talmud from memory.  Yehudah does not disclose whether it was Talmud Bavli or Talmud Yerushalmi – yet this writer presumes Talmud Bavli since Yehuday “Natronai” ben Naḥman Gaon used Talmud Bavli.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the time of Natronai ibn Hakhinai’s emergence as leader in Carcassonne/Narbonne, the Syrian Junds arrived to fight under Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayri who was a Syrian Arab military commander in North Africa and Iberia, and br
2 CONC iefly ruler of al-Andalus in 742 in order to “Orientalize” southern Spain – from Cordoba. Shortly after control of al-Andalus was turned over to the Syrian Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayri, Arabian Umayyah bin Abdul Malik and Qatn bin A
2 CONC bdul Malik (both sons of Abdul Malik ibn Qatn Fahr) started making secret plans against Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayri.  Yusuf bin Abdur Rahman, the Governor of Narbonne along with the Arabized Berbers all agreed join the two sons to 
2 CONC battle against Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayri.  Two enormous Muslim armies of over 10,000 men each, went to war.  Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayri was seriously injured and died shortly thereafter. The Syrian named Balj ibn Bishr al-Qushayr
2 CONC i ruled al-Andalus for eleven months.  After his death, Syrians, Berbers and Arabs jointly agreed upon a Yemenite, Thalba bin Salamah, as new ruler of al-Andalus.  This didn’t work out well for either Arabs of Yemenites.  Thalba b
2 CONC in Salamah was deposed within a year.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Yusuf bin Abdur Rahman Fihri, the Governor of Narbonne, was appointed Ruler of al-Andalus in 751 CE.  He split al-Andalus into four provinces while the French Territories became the fifth province:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Andalusia	Corbova, Carmona, Seville, Sidonia, Malaga, Ilbira, Jaen 2.	Toledo		Ubeda, Boeza, Marbella, Villena, Valencia 3.	Merida		Merida, Lisbon, Badajoz, Salamanca 4.	Saragossa	Saragossa, Tarragona, Barcelona, Lerida 5.	Arbon
2 CONC ia		Narbonne, Tolousse, Pamplona, Lugo
2 CONT
2 CONT This marked the rise of Abassids over Syrians.  But please note that a Syrian presence in Nasrid controlled Granada, al-Andalus, is documented until expulsion in 1492.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Father of Natronai, ''''Habibai' ben Natronai, Exilarch of Pumbeditha & Carcassonne''' was born abt 683 in Pumbedita, Babylon, yet died 775 CE in Saint Etienne, Loire, Rhone-Alpes, France.  Habibai was elected leader of the Je
2 CONC ws of Carcassone.  When Nasrids attacked Carcassone,  Habibai contacted the Duke of Marseilles for help – none was offered.  Habibai contacted Charles Martel and enlisted his help thus pledging organized Jewish support for Charles
2 CONC  Martel to destroy Marseilles and stop the advance of Syrian Yusuf bin Abdar Rahman; later, in preparation for the Battle of Poitiers in the Loire Valley of France, Habibai would perform the same function for Charles Martel.  So b
2 CONC egins the allegiance between Jews of Narbonne and the Carolingian Empire – and opposition to both by Muslims and Jews of Arab lands (Syria notably).  This event set the stage for later relations between the Jews of Andalusia (of a
2 CONC rab lands) and Jews of Catalonia, France and Germany (of christian lands).
2 CONT
2 CONT “The History of Islam, Volume 3” By Akbar Shāh K̲h̲ān Najībābādī, Ṣafī al-Raḥmān Mubārakfūrī, Abdul Rahman Abdullah
