1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abū 'l-Kathīr Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyāʾ
2 CONT
2 CONT Abū ʾl-Kathīr Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyāʾ (d. ca. 932) was a Jewish theologian and Bible translator from Tiberias whose main claim to fame is the fact that Saʿadya Gaon studied with him at some point. He is not mentioned in any Jewish so
2 CONC urce, and apart from the Andalusian heresiographer and polemicist Ibn Ḥazm (d. 1064), who mentions him as a Jewish mutakallim (free-thinking theologian), our main source of information is Kitāb al-Tanbīh (ed. De Goeje, 1894) by th
2 CONC e well-known Muslim historian al-Masʿūdī (d. 956). In his brief survey of Arabic translations of the Bible, al-Masʿūdī states that the Israelites rely for exegesis and translation of the Hebrew books—i.e., the Torah, Prophets, and
2 CONC  Psalms, twenty-four books in all, he says—on a number of Israelites whom they praise highly, almost all of whom he has met in person. He mentions Abū ʾl-Kathīr as one of them, and also Saʿadya, whose name al-Masʿūdī gives as Saʿī
2 CONC d ibn Yaʿqūb (as opposed to Yūsuf) al-Fayyūmī.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Masʿūdī adds that he and Abū ʾl-Kathīr often engaged in disputations in Palestine and Jordan. These covered a range of topics, but the only one he specifies is the question of the abrogation of revealed laws and the difference 
2 CONC between abrogation (Ar. naskh) and changes occurring in the divine will as a result of the emergence of new circumstances. Although al-Masʿūdī does not mention his own view on abrogation, it may be assumed that he subscribed to th
2 CONC e general Muslim position, which is that Jewish law was superseded and invalidated first by Christianity, and thereafter by Islam, and that no change in the divine will is implied, since God had decided in advance how long each se
2 CONC t of precepts would be in force.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unclear whether Saʿadya participated in the discussions with the other the two, but he discusses the issue of abrogation in great detail in his ''Kitāb al-Amānāt wa ʾl-Iʿtiqādāt'' (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions), in which
2 CONC  he defends the eternal validity of the Torah and refutes, on scriptural grounds, the Muslim (and Christian) claim of its abrogation. The extent of Abū ʾl-Kathīr’s influence on Saʿadya’s thought cannot be established, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abū ʾl-Kathīr’s profession is also unclear. al-Masʿūdī calls him a kātib , which has been variously interpreted as secretary, government official, (biblical) scribe, Masorete, and book copyist. For lack of further information, som
2 CONC e scholars have tried to identify Abū ʾl-Kathīr with the Hebrew grammarian Abū ʿAlī Judah ben ʿAllān, likewise of Tiberias, who seems to have been a Karaite. However, al-Masūdī unequivocally describes Abu ʾl-Kathīr, as well as his
2 CONC  student Saʿadya, as an ashmaʿathī, that is, a Rabbanite.
2 CONT
2 CONT Camilla Adang
2 CONT
