2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Sherira bar Hanina''' (Hebrew: שרירא בר חנינא) more commonly known as Sherira Gaon (Hebrew: שרירא גאון; c. 906-c. 1006) was the Gaon of the Academy of Pumbeditha. He was one of the most prominent Geonim of his period, and the f
2 CONC ather of Hai Gaon, who succeeded him as Gaon. He wrote the Iggeret Rav Sherira Gaon ("[The] Epistle of Rav Sherira Gaon"), a comprehensive history of the composition of the Talmud.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents 1	Life 2	His Responsa 3	His circular letter 4	See also 5	References 6	External links Life Sherira was born circa 906 C.E., the descendant, both on his father's and his mother's side, of prominent families, several members
2 CONC  of which had occupied the gaonate. His father was Hananiah ben R. Yehudai, also a gaon. Sherira claimed descent from Rabbah b. Abuha, who belonged to the family of the exilarch, thereby claiming descent from the Davidic line. She
2 CONC rira stated that his genealogy could be traced back to the pre-Bostanaian branch of that family, which, he claimed, on account of the deterioration of the exilarchate had renounced its claims thereto, preferring instead the schola
2 CONC r's life.[3] The seal of his family was a lion, which was said to have been the emblem of the Judean kings.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira officiated first as chief judge. While in that office, he refused to recognize the election of Nehemiah ben Kohen Tzedek as gaon in 960. On Nehemiah's death in 968 C.E., Sherira was elected gaon of the Academy of Pumbedita
2 CONC , soon after which he appointed his son, Hai, chief judge in his stead.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 997, he and his son were maliciously denounced by enemies to the caliph al-Qadir, though the nature of the accusation is unknown. He and his son were imprisoned and deprived of their property, even of the necessaries of life. T
2 CONC hough the incarceration was brief, Sherira was now in very bad health. Sherira resigned the gaonate in 998 C.E., appointing his son as his successor.[2] Sherira died soon after, circa 1006 C.E.[5] He was the alleged father in law 
2 CONC of Elijah ben Menahem Ha-Zaken.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT His Responsa As director of the academy he sought to reach pupils both near and far, and many of his responsa have been preserved in the geonic collections and in the works containing the earlier decisions. His responsa are simila
2 CONC r to the geonic responsa in general, a majority of them dealing with questions of religious practise, though some of them contain expositions and comments on passages of the Talmud and the Mishnah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Indeed, his literary activity was confined to Talmudic and to related subjects. He was not greatly interested in Arabic literature, although he knew enough Arabic to be able to write in that language those of his decisions that we
2 CONC re addressed to communities in Muslim countries. Generally he preferred to use Hebrew or Aramaic for that purpose.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira was noted for the nobility and seriousness of his character. As a judge he endeavored to arrive at the exact facts of a case and to render his decisions in strict conformity with the Law. In deciding practical questions he
2 CONC  adopted the more rigorous view, following the letter of the Talmud with the purpose of upholding and emphasizing its authority against the attacks of the Karaites. He frequently formulates in his responsa rules which are highly i
2 CONC mportant for the correct interpretation of the Talmud. For instance, he declares that the term "mitzvah" designates in some passages a command that may not be broken with impunity, but in other passages denotes merely an admonitio
2 CONC n with which it would be commendable to comply, but which may be disregarded without fear of punishment.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira is thought by some to have been a student of kabbalistic mysticism; but when asked about the mystical works "Shi'ur Komah" and "Hekalot" and whether they represented ancient traditions (originating with R. Ishmael and R. A
2 CONC kiva), he replied in a responsum[8] that the passage in "Shi'ur Ḳomah" ascribing human organs to God embodies profound mysteries, but must not be taken literally. Sherira wrote a work on the Talmud, under the title "Megillat Setar
2 CONC im." In this work he seems to have discussed the importance of the aggadah;[9] but the portion of the work containing his opinions on this subject has been lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT His circular letter Main article: Iggeret of Rabbi Sherira Gaon Sherira's seminal work for which he is most renowned is his Epistle, or Iggeret, written to Rabbi Jacob ben Nissim of Kairouan, and where he addressed the question of
2 CONC  how the Talmud was formulated, and brings down a chronological list of the geonim who officiated in Babylonia during the period of the Exilarchs (Resh Galutha). This Iggeret is considered by many to be one of the classics in Jewi
2 CONC sh historiography.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT See also Geonim References
