2 CONC holar's life (Letter of Sherira Gaon, in Neubauer, l.c. i. 23, 33).
2 CONT
2 CONT The seal of his family was a lion, which was said to have been the emblem of the Judean kings (Ibn Daud, l.c.).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Talmud has numerous references to sages who were said to be descendants of King David or “from the House of David.” This is particularly noticeable in references to Hillel “the Elder” (circa 100 BCE), who is called a descendan
2 CONC t of Shephatiah, son of Abital (tractates Ketubot and Taanit).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixteen generations of his descendants were called “Nasi” (prince) up to approximately 425 CE. The Talmud names only two or three generations of sages and is helpful in identification, but not in tracing genealogies.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important source is Iggeret Rav Sherira Gaon (The Letter of Rabbi Sherira Gaon), written in 986 CE. This was an early responsum by the head of the Talmudic Academy of Pumbedita in Babylonia. He was one of the dynasty of exilarc
2 CONC hs (Resh Galuta, or Prince of the Exiles), who claimed descent from King David.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second part of the Letter discusses the exilarchs and the heads of the academies in Sura and Pumbedita. Rabbi Sherira attempts to distinguish between facts and stories and presents an early post-biblical history written largel
2 CONC y in Aramaic. This Letter serves as a major source of the chronology and genealogy of the Geonim.
2 CONT
2 CONT The institutions of the exilarch began with the descendants of Jehoiachin, last King of Judah, who was exiled to Babylonia. It survived until 825 CE with Jews continuing to view the exilarch as their leader for several centuries a
2 CONC fter its decline.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira's important Letter brings the Davidic genealogy to his time. <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> "Babylonian academy head (gaon), Sherira ben Hanina came from a noble family
2 CONT
2 CONT which claimed to trace its ancestry to King David.
2 CONT
