1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jekuthiel ibn Ḥasan was a Jewish communal leader, statesman, philanthropist, and patron of the arts who served in the Muslim court of the Banū Tujīb in Saragossa during the period of the party kings (Ar. mulūk al-ṭ
2 CONC awā’if). He may have been a member of the Ibn Qapron family from Cordova, and it seems very likely that he studied philosophy in addition to traditional Jewish learning; he even apparently wrote some poetry. One of the young Jewis
2 CONC h poets and scholars he supported was Solomon ibn Gabirol. The esteem between the two men was mutual and very deep. Ibn Gabirol praised his benefactor in several panegyrics, giving him the title of nasi.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1030s Jekuthiel held a high office in the court of the taifa monarch Yaḥyā ibn al-Mundhir II. He was killed in 1039, at an advanced age, during the turmoil in the city when the Banū Hūd took control of the kingdom. Ibn Gabi
2 CONC rol lamented Jekuthiel’s death in three elegies. In a short, beautiful poem, Reʾeh Shemesh le-ʿEt ʿErev Adumma (Heb. See the Sun Red at Eventide), his personal sorrow is paralleled in the darkness of the skies, a reflection of the
2 CONC  cosmic mourning. The longest of the elegies, Bime	 Yequti’el Asher Nigmaru (Heb. In the Days of Jekuthiel, Which Have Come to an End), has a more formal and national tone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angel Saénz-Badillos
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
