1 NOTE Yoseph was assassinated in a pogrom in Granada (1062) where he served as a Grand Vizier like his father before him
2 CONT R. Yoseph I descends from King David <biblical.html> in one of two possible ways. According to The ESKELES Genealogy <esk.html> by Zeev ESHKOLOT <esk1a.html> he was the son of Rav Khai (Hai) Gaon <kings.html> (b. 939, d. 1038 CE).
2 CONC  See p. 320 and 1130 of vol. 7 of Encyclopedia Judaica <http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0685362531/loebfamilytree>. (See Aryeh LIFSCHEUTZ, Avoth Atarah le-Banim, Warsaw (1927) p. 163 for claim of descent from R. Hai.)
2 CONT However, in Hai's eulogy, Samuel Ha-Nagid said that Hai left no child. (!?) Perhaps this simply meant that Samuel's son R. Yoseph I had already passed away.
2 CONT Alternatively, According to David HUGHES <http://hometown.aol.com/rdavidh218/davidicdynasty.html>, RDavidH218 at Aol.com <mailto:RDavidH218 at Aol.com>, Yoseph was rather the son of Samuel Ha-Nagid <hophni.html>, and Hai married a
2 CONC  daughter of Samuel Ha-Nagid. (Gaon of the Sura academy 997-1013).
