1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Qaid ibn Hammad (died 1054) was the second Hammadid ruler in what is now Algeria. He succeeded his father Hammad ibn Buluggin in 1028. He named his brother Yusuf as governor of North Africa, and another brother, Ou
2 CONC ighlan, governor of Hamza. In 1038 he was attacked by Hammama, lord of Fes, but pushed him back. Four years later, he signed a treaty of peace with the Zirid al-Muizz ibn Badis, who had moved against him from Kairouan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1048, when al-Muizz declared himself subject of the Abbasid caliph of Baghdad, Qaid confirmed his allegiance to the Fatimid caliphs of Egypt, obtaining by caliph Ma'ad al-Mustansir Billah the title of Sharaf al-Dawla. He died i
2 CONC n 1045, and was succeeded by his son Muhsin ibn Qaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Qaid_ibn_Hammad
