1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ibn al-Majjānī Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ibn al-Majjānī family, known from documentary sources in the Cairo Geniza, were active in Mediterranean trade during the first half of the eleventh century. The earliest member of the family for whom any correspondence survive
2 CONC s was Mūsā (Abū ʿImrān) ibn Yaḥyā al-Majjānī. The nisba indicates that the family once resided in the Tunisian town of Majjāna. Goitein suggested that this pertained to Mūsā’s grandfather (Med. Soc., vol 1, p. 371, no. 9), from wh
2 CONC ose hand there are three letters (Gil, nos. 116–18) dated respectively 1000 (from Fustat), 1011 (from Qayrawan), and 1025 (from Alexandria). These and other letters make it evident that Mūsā lived in Qayrawan, where he was the lea
2 CONC ding partner/agent, both in that city and perhaps generally, of the preeminent Egyptian merchant Joseph ibn ʿAwkal. After Mūsā’s death in June 1039, the family’s business was taken over by his son Yaḥyā, from whom we have four Gen
2 CONC iza letters (Gil, nos. 627–630), and after Yaḥyā’s death in 1056, by Mūsā’s grandson and namesake Mūsā (ben Judah) ibn Yaḥyā al-Majjānī, from whom we have one Geniza letter (Gil, no. 634; ca. 1052). The al-Majjānīs were related by
2 CONC  marriage to the  Tāhirtīs  (the wives of Yaḥyā II al-Majjānī and Ṣāliḥ al-Tāhirtī were sisters), another prominent merchant family of Qayrawan, and through them, therefore, to the sons of Berechiah, Joseph, and Nissim (in a lette
2 CONC r written ca. 1015, these latter refer to Abū ʿImrān (Mūsā I) al-Majjānī as “our in-law and our partner”). Also surviving in the Geniza are three letters from Mūsā’s (Abū ʿImrān’s) brother Isaac ben ʿAlī (ʿAllūsh) al-Majjānī (Gil,
2 CONC  nos. 635–637).
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael G. Wechsler
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben-Sasson, Menahem. The Emergence of the Local Jewish Community in the Muslim World: Qayrawan, 800–1057, 2nd ed. (Jerusalem: Magnes Press, 1997) [Hebrew].
2 CONT
2 CONT Gil, Moshe. In the Kingdom of Ishmael, 4 vols. (Tel Aviv: Tel Aviv University, 1997) [Hebrew]; vol. 1 trans.  D. Strassler as Jews in Islamic Countries in the Middle Ages (Leiden: Brill, 2004).
2 CONT
