1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ezra ben Abraham ben Mazhir
2 CONT
2 CONT Ezra ha-Kohen ben Abraham ben Mazhir was head of the rump Palestinian Yeshiva in Damascus from 1164 to 1191. He was apparently a son-in-law, but not a direct descendant, of the House of Ha-Kohen that had headed the venerable yeshi
2 CONC va, with interruptions, since 1025. By his time the yeshiva was in a serious decline, having been forced to move from Jerusalem to Tyre in 1071 and to Damascus around 1097. In view of the Seljuq and Crusader conquests, the yeshiva
2 CONC  under Maṣliaḥ ha-Kohen ben Solomon was finally compelled to relocate to Egypt, which was still under Fatimid rule. Some members of the yeshiva stayed behind in Damascus under the leadership of Ezra ha-Kohen.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Petahiah of Regensburg’s account of his journey to Baghdad and Syria in the twelfth century, Ezra received his ordination from Samuel ben Eli, the head of the Baghdad yeshiva; the fact that the Palestinian yeshiva had
2 CONC  to rely on the Baghdad yeshiva demonstrates the extent of its decline. Samuel ben Eli’s letters preserved in the Cairo Geniza also mention Ezra, as well as his brother Sar Shalom, the sons of Abraham, the head of the Yeshivat Ge’
2 CONC on Yaʿaqov (Heb. Yeshiva of the Pride of Jacob). Samuel ben Eli was in close contact with Ezra ben Abraham, and they supported one another against the exilarch Daniel ben Ḥasday, who vehemently opposed granting any authority to th
2 CONC e Damascus yeshiva in matters of ordination in Egypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no doubt that the appointment of Nathaniel ha-Levi ben Moses as gaon in Egypt by the exilarch Daniel ben Ḥasdai, and the appointment of Ezra ben Abraham as gaon in Syria by Samuel ben Eli, head of the Baghdad yeshiva is p
2 CONC roof of the difficult relations in Baghdad between the head of the yeshiva and the exilarch. This discord planted the seeds of the conflict between the gaon Samuel ben Eli in Baghdad, and Maimonides in Cairo, who sided with the ex
2 CONC ilarchs. One of the issues that led to the clash between Samuel ben Eli and Maimonides pertained to the laws of the Sabbath, specifically those surrounding river travel. Both of them wrote about this topic to Abraham ha-Kohen of B
2 CONC aghdad, most probably Ezra’s father, each of them seeking to convince him of the validity of their arguments. In the end, the Damascus yeshiva disintegrated, and what remained of it moved to Egypt, which was already under Ayyubid 
2 CONC rule at the end of the twelfth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elinoar Bareket
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Gil, Moshe. Jews in Islamic Countries in the Middle Ages (Leiden: Brill, 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT Strauss [-Ashtor], Eliyahu. A History of the Jews in Egypt and Syria under the Rule of the Mamlūks, 3 vols. (Jerusalem: Mosad Harav Kook, 1944).
2 CONT
