1 NOTE {geni:about_me} source: http://www.geni.com/people/Judah-ben-Solomon-al-%E1%B8%A4ar%C4%ABz%C4%AB/6000000022453877614?through=6000000022453945754
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yehuda_Alharizi
2 CONT
2 CONT Yehuda Alharizi, also Judah ben Solomon Harizi or al-Harizi (Hebrew: יהודה בן שלמה אלחריזי‎, Yehudah ben Shelomo al-Harizi, Arabic: يحيا بن سليمان بن شاؤل أبو زكريا الحريزي اليهودي من أهل طليطلة‎, Yahya bin Sulaiman bin Sha'ul abu
2 CONC  Zakaria al-Harizi al-Yahudi min ahl Tulaitila) was a rabbi, translator, poet and traveller active in Spain in the Middle Ages (1165 in Toledo? – 1225 in Aleppo). He was supported by wealthy patrons, to whom he wrote poems and ded
2 CONC icated compositions.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a rationalist, conveying the works of Maimonides and his approach to rationalistic Judaism. He translated Maimonides' Guide for the Perplexed and some of his Commentary on the Mishnah, as well as the Mahbarot Iti'el of the 
2 CONC Arab poet al-Hariri, from the Arabic to Hebrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alharizi's poetic translation of the Guide for the Perplexed is considered by many to be more readable than that of Samuel ben Judah ibn Tibbon. However, it has not been very widely used in Jewish scholarship, perhaps because it i
2 CONC s less precise. It had some influence in the Christian world due to its translation into Latin.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alharizi's own works include the "Tahkemoni", composed between 1218 and 1220, in the Arabic form known as maqama. This is written in Hebrew in unmetrical rhymes, in what is commonly termed rhymed prose. It is a series of humorous 
2 CONC episodes, witty verses, and quaint applications of Scriptural texts. The episodes are bound together by the presence of the hero and of the narrator, who is also the author. Another collection of his poetry was devoted to preachin
2 CONC g ethical self-discipline and fear of heaven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harizi undertook long journeys in the lands of the Middle East. His works are suffused with his impressions from these journeys.
2 CONT
2 CONT He not only brought to perfection the art of applying Hebrew to secular satire, but he was also a brilliant literary critic and his maqama on the Andalusian Hebrew poets is a fruitful source of information.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Judah al-Ḥarīzī (ca. 1166–1225) was a central author of medieval Hebrew, Judeo-Arabic, and classical Arabic literature as well as an important translator of Arabic and Judeo-Arabic literary, legal, and philosophical texts into Heb
2 CONC rew. Born in Toledo, then still a highly Arabized city in the Christian kingdom of Castile, al-Ḥarizi left around 1208 to travel to Provence and the Islamic East, going as far as Baghdad and ultimately settling in Ayyubid Aleppo, 
2 CONC where he died. Information concerning al-Ḥarizi’s place and date of death, his uncommon height, and his Maghrebi accent is recorded in the biographical dictionary of poets by Ibn al-Shaʿār al-Mawṣalī (1197–1256), entitled Qalāʾid 
2 CONC al-Jumān fī Farāʾid Shuʿarāʾ hādha ʾl-Zemān. In the dictionary, al-Mawṣalī gives al-Ḥarīzī’s full Arabic name as Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān ibn Shaʾūl Abū Zakariyya al-Ḥarīzī al-Yahūdī.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Ḥarīzī’s translations of Judeo-Arabic and Arabic halakhic, philosophical, and belletristic works were generally commissioned by Jewish intellectuals in Christian Iberia and Provence. Among the works he translated were Moses Ibn
2 CONC  Ezra’s Maqālat al-Ḥadīqa fī Maʿnā al-Majāz wa ʾl-Ḥaqīqa (Treatise of the Garden of Figurative and Literal Speech), entitled Arugat ha-Bosem (The Gardenbed of Spice) in Hebrew; Maimonides’s Dalālat al-Ḥāʾirīn (Guide of the Perplex
2 CONC ed), entitled Moreh Nevukhim in Hebrew (this translation was written after Samuel Ibn Tibbon’s more canonical, authorized translation; it was commissioned by readers in Provence who desired a less opaque register of Hebrew); Maimo
2 CONC nides’s Introduction to the Mishna and his commentary on the first five tractates of Zera‛im; Maimonides’s Essay on Resurrection; ʿAlī ibn Riḍwān’s Epistle on Morals, entitled Iggeret ʿAli ha-Yishmaʾeli (The Epistle of ʿAlī the Is
2 CONC hmaelite) in Hebrew; Ḥunayn ibn Isḥāq’s Ādāb al-Falāsifa (Dicta of the Philosophers), entitled Musre ha-Filosofim in Hebrew; Galen’s Dialogue on the Soul; and the Arabic Maqāmāt (rhymed-prose fictional narratives) of al-Ḥarīrī of 
2 CONC Basra, entitled Maḥberot Ittiʾel (Ithiel’s Compositions) in Hebrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT The method for the translation of nonbelletristic texts adopted by al-Ḥarizi tends to preserve the purity of Hebrew diction, sometimes at the expense of accuracy, which places it in contrast with the contemporaneous literalist met
2 CONC hod emerging in Provence. In Maḥberot Ittiʾel, he tends to biblicize and Judaize the text by writing in biblical Hebrew, borrowing figures of speech from the Bible (often out of context for ironic or humorous purposes), resetting 
2 CONC stories in biblical locations, and replacing Islamic cultural and religious references with Jewish ones. For example, in al-Ḥarīrī’s twelfth maqāma, which is set in Ghutah, near Damascus, a drunk preacher disguised as a Sufi mendi
2 CONC cant teaches a prayer for protection opening with the first sura of the Qur’ān and including blessings for the Prophet Muḥammad. In al-Ḥarīzī’s rewriting, the story is set in the “Heights of Naphtali and Asher” in the Land of Isra
2 CONC el, praise for Muḥammad is replaced with praise for Moses, and the qur’anic sura is replaced with Psalm 91, which appropriately is a prayer for protection. At the same time, al-Ḥarīzī preserves the literary conceits of al-Ḥarīrī w
2 CONC henever possible. For example, for his translation of al-Ḥarīrī’s sixteenth maqāma, which includes palindromes, al-Ḥarīzī composed original Hebrew palindromes rather than render the Arabic literally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, Maḥberot Itti’el does not preserve an introduction that might have elucidated the author’s methods and motivations for the translation at the time of its composition. However, an explanation is given, albeit in hind
2 CONC sight, in al-Ḥarīzī’s introduction to Sefer Taḥkemoni, which was composed after he had left Spain to wander the Islamic East. He writes that he undertook Maḥberot Ittiʾel as a challenge posed by some Spanish Jews who had become en
2 CONC amored with the maqāmāt of al-Ḥarīrī and thought them untranslatable. Al-Ḥarīzī thus sought to mitigate the awe-inspiring status of al-Ḥarīrī’s text by demonstrating its translatability. He also states in the introduction to Sefer
2 CONC  Taḥkemoni that he regretted having translated al-Ḥarīrī’s maqāmāt because “I went to translate a foreign book as though the words of the Living God were not among us; I rushed to tend a foreign vineyard but did not tend my own.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Sefer Taḥkemoni was explicitly composed in competition with al-Ḥarīrī’s maqāmāt and to establish Hebrew’s versatility, eloquence, and superiority over Arabic. Al-Ḥarīzī even suggests that the seeming richness of Arabic is owed to 
2 CONC tropes plagiarized from Hebrew and assures his readers that nothing in his Hebrew book is taken from al-Ḥarīrī. Given this, it may seem surprising that Sefer Taḥkemoni borrows liberally from Arabic plot materials in maqāma collect
2 CONC ions by Badīʿ al-Zamān al-Hamadhānī, and al-Ḥarīrī as well as from other Arabic sources. The difficulty may be resolvable if one thinks of originality as pertaining to turns of phrase and figures of speech rather than plot.
2 CONT
2 CONT The title Taḥkemoni is taken from the name of one of King David’s warriors in II Samuel 23:8. The root of the name, ḥkm, means “to be wise”; thus the title conveys both might and wisdom. Like the classical Arabic maqāma collection
2 CONC s by al-Hamadhānī and al-Ḥarīrī, Sefer Taḥkemoni’s chapters involve interactions between two characters, a narrator and a protagonist rogue. These characters (and other characters too) possess symbolic names whose sense is reveale
2 CONC d through semantic and intertextual analysis. The narrator is Heman the Ezrahite (Ezraḥi), a name that appears in this precise form as the author of Psalm 88. There is another Heman whose wisdom is compared with that of King Solom
2 CONC on (I Kings 5:11) and yet a third Heman called Heman the poet (I Chronicles 6:18). The narrator’s name thus denotes wisdom and poetry. On a semantic level, Ezraḥi is derived from the root zrḥ, meaning “to rise from the soil.“ Thus
2 CONC , it is not surprising that the narrator reveals his native land (i.e., the soil from which he rises) to be Spain (Sefarad), also the native soil of the author.
2 CONT
2 CONT The narrator’s status as “one who rises from the soil” places him in contrast with the protagonist, Heber the Kenite (Ḥever ha-Qeni), the namesake of a character from Judges 4:11 who “pitched his tent at Elon be-Ṣaʿananim.” He is 
2 CONC called Ḥever because he “brings together” (from the root ḥbr) scattered bits of rhetoric and teaching. His tribe is the Kenites, descendants of the biblical Cain, who was cursed to wander after killing his brother Abel. While his 
2 CONC home, Elon be-Ṣaʿananim, is a simple place-name in the Bible, it is significant here because of the force of its root ṣʿn, “to wander.” Thus, he comes from a place of abstract and eternal wandering.
2 CONT
2 CONT The constant movement from place to place in the Taḥkemoni imbues the text with the ideal of wanderlust common in maqāma literature. There is probably a relationship between al-Ḥarīzī’s selection of the maqāma as his genre of choi
2 CONC ce and his own experience of wandering. The forty-sixth chapter details the journey of Heber the Kenite from Spain through Provence, the Land of Israel, and the lands of the Islamic East, concluding in Baghdad. Largely believed to
2 CONC  be autobiographical, the chapter preserves the identities of many historical figures whom al-Ḥarīzī undoubtedly met, either lavishing them with praise or censuring them with blame. This Hebrew chapter has strong parallels with a 
2 CONC Judeo-Arabic maqāma, discussed further below, that has been associated with al-Ḥarīzī.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two other original Hebrew compositions by al-Ḥarīzī can be identified with certainty. In the brief Iggeret Leshon ha-Zahav (Epistle of the Golden Tongue), a female speaker identifying herself as Wisdom (Heb. ḥokhma) and the Golden
2 CONC  Tongue (Heb. leshon ha-zahav) instruct the author concerning the physical world, the nature of the soul, and similar matters. This piece bears a significant resemblance to a scene in the introduction to Sefer Taḥkemoni in which t
2 CONC he Hebrew tongue speaks to the author as a woman; the Epistle was likely composed prior to the Taḥkemoni. Sefer ha-‘Anaq (Book of the Necklace) is a collection of homonymic epigrams on devotional and moralistic themes. The book bo
2 CONC rrows its homonymic form, though not its content, from a book of the same title by Moses ibn Ezra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two Judeo-Arabic compositions and one personal letter also survive from al-Ḥarizi’s pen. Al-Rawḍa al-Anīqa (The Elegant Garden) is a maqāma mentioned by al-Mawṣalī that mostly likely contained the author’s address to the Jews of B
2 CONC aghdad for not appreciating him sufficiently. This work seems to have been incorporated into the longer Kitāb al-Durar (Book of Pearls), which describes al-Ḥarīzī’s journeys in the Islamic East more broadly and opens with Judeo-Ar
2 CONC abic poems in praise of God. Kitāb al-Durar has been associated with al-Ḥarīzī because of the many details that overlap with chapter 46 of Sefer Taḥkemoni, though there are notable variations, such as the order in which cities are
2 CONC  presented and the inclusion of additional stopping places during the journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT The aforementioned biographical dictionary by al-Mawṣali also preserves six poems by al-Ḥarīzī in classical Arabic. These include an erotic poem based on a verse by the Andalusian poet-prince al-Muʿatamad Ibn ʿAbbād (d. 1095) and 
2 CONC several panegyrics dedicated to Ayyubid rulers, including al-Mālik al-Ashraf ibn Abū Bakr Ayyūb (d. 1237), who seem to have supported al-Ḥarīzī in his final days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan P. Decter
2 CONT
