1 NAME David /ben Samuel/
2 GIVN David
2 _MARNM ben Samuel
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} David ben Samuel
2 CONT
2 CONT In a pair of documents dated 1197 and 1201, “ David, the head of the diasporas of all Israel,” appoints a father and son as beadles in the synagogue of Ezra the Scribe in Baghdad. These are the only known references to an exilarch
2 CONC  whose name is given simply as David. According to Jacob Mann, this David was the son of Samuel of Mosul, one of two cousins whom Petahiah of Regensburg describes as competing for the exilarchate in the last quarter of the twelfth
2 CONC  century. In Mann’s view, Samuel prevailed over his cousin, served as exilarch, and was ultimately succeeded by his son David, who obviously died sometime after 1201.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold Franklin Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Gil, Moshe. The	 Jews in Islamic Countries in the Middle Ages (Leiden: Brill, 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT Goode, Alexander. “The Exilarchate in the Eastern Caliphate, 637–1258,” Jewish Quarterly Review, n.s. 31 (1940): 149–169.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann, Jacob. Texts and Studies in Jewish History and Literature (repr. New York: Ktav, 1972).
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold Franklin. "David ben Samuel." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online, 2013. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference. Jim Harlow. 30 August 2013 <http://brillonline.nl/entries/encyclopedia-of-jews-in-the-islamic-world/david-ben-samuel-SIM_0006410>
