2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/ac/de/0b/31/5344483d5c392916/beatrice_gracia_nasi_original.jpg?hash=6384eb048a1e7d3ebedea2cb1318c030fe8d78588d42dc3c43cac193665dbb6f.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE This uniface medal was created by Pastorino de’Pastorini in 1557-58. At left is the sitter’s name in Hebrew characters, and at right is a Latin inscription “A[nno] AE[tas] XVIII” (in the year of her age eighteen). During the Renai
3 CONC ssance, medals became a significant art form as men and women sought to emulate the Romans of antiquity. In imitation of antique coins bearing portraits of Roman emperors, Renaissance rulers and church officials, as well as member
3 CONC s of the lesser nobility and the bourgeoisie, commissioned idealized likenesses that expressed their virtú (character) and glorified their personalities. Generally, the individual was depicted in a bust portrait that gave an ennob
3 CONC led version of his or her appearance. The surrounding inscription identifies the sitter and may give additional information, as on this example. Since Gracia Nasi, the younger, is known to have been born in 1540, the inscription g
3 CONC iving her age as eighteen indicates a date of 1557-58 for the medal.
3 CONT
3 CONT Pastorino de’Pastorini was a well-known medalist who produced several similar portrait medals. Many of them show the subject surrounded by a beaded border and an inscription like those seen on Gracia Nasi’s medal, but no other bea
3 CONC rs a Hebrew inscription. Since Pastorino never worked outside Italy, this medal must have been commissioned before the sitter left for Istanbul in 1558.
3 CONT
3 CONT Source: http://thejewishmuseum.org/collection/12997-medal-of-gracia-nasi-the-younger
