1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Solomon Ben Yaʿesh (also Ibn Yaʿish or Abenæs and Alvaro Mendès) was born in Tavira, Portugal in 1520 and died in Istanbul in 1603. A wealthy and influential Jewish statesman and diplomat, he worked to stymie Spain
2 CONC  at the height of its power by engineering an alliance between England and the Ottoman Empire. The man who later received the title of duke of Mytilene was born to a marrano (anusim) family in Portugal as Alvaro Mendès, a name whi
2 CONC ch most Europeans would continue to use with him throughout his life. In his youth, Mendès worked as an apprentice to a goldsmith before going to India in 1545 to manage his parents’ affairs and making his own large fortune in the
2 CONC  diamond mines of the kingdom of Narsinga, near present-day Chennai (formerly Madras).
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his return to Portugal, Mendès became an intimate of King João III (r. 1521– 1557), who made him a knight of the Order of Santiago (Saint James of Compostela). Following the death of his patron João, Mendès moved to Madrid fo
2 CONC r seven years. The increasing strength of the Inquisition led him to move to Florence in 1564 and then to Paris in 1569; he lived also in Antwerp, London, and Venice for brief periods thereafter. During this time, he established c
2 CONC lose ties with leading figures in Europe, including Elizabeth I  of England (r. 1558–1603), Henri III of France (r. 1574–1589), and the latter’s mother, Catherine de’ Medici (1519–1589). Mendès joined them in their support for the
2 CONC  Portuguese pretender António (1531–1595), who challenged Felipe II of Spain (r. 1556–1598) for the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in 1585, Mendès traveled to the Ottoman Empire in order to obtain assistance for António’s cause. Such was his status that he was given a distinguished safe conduct and escort. Upon his arrival, he decided to settle there pe
2 CONC rmanently and, together with his household, returned to Judaism. Now known as Solomon Ben Yaʿesh, he soon established close relations with Sultan Murad III (r. 1574–1595), who appointed him governor of Mytilene (with the Italian t
2 CONC itle of duke), a lucrative post that reputedly brought him an income of around 18,000 ducats. Likewise, he was granted a customs-revenue farm reportedly valued at 1,000 ducats daily that went to pay the Janissary Corps; he put his
2 CONC  nephew Jacob Anes in charge of its management. The sultan held Ben Yaʿesh in great esteem, and his successor, Mehmed III (r. 1595–1603), retained him as a close confidant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout his Ottoman career, Ben Yaʿesh labored to bring about an alliance between England and the Ottoman Empire against Spain. To this end, he arranged for Elizabeth’s representative, William Harboune (ca. 1542–1617), to come 
2 CONC to Istanbul as English ambassador and made use of his intelligence network in Europe to keep the Ottomans informed of continental happenings. The community of marranos in London assisted him by passing information about Iberia to 
2 CONC the court of Elizabeth. In this way, Ben Yaʿesh succeeded, in his own words, in preventing the Ottomans from concluding a peace with Spain and isolating England. His brother-in-law Roderigo Lopez was the personal physician to Eliz
2 CONC abeth and became a conduit for correspondence between Istanbul and London. When the Spanish Armada was defeated in 1588, Ben Yaʿesh received word and informed the Sublime Porte even before the English ambassador.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben Yaʿesh’s success met with both jealousy and criticism. The English ambassador, Edward Barton (1533?– 1598), fell out with him soon after he took up his post in Istanbul in 1588 and repeatedly sought to defame his reputation. S
2 CONC imilarly, when António’s incompetence led to a break with Ben Yaʿesh, he falsely accused falsely Ben Yaʿesh of cheating him. To counter the libelous allegations by Barton and António, Ben Yaʿesh sent his agent Solomon Kormano to d
2 CONC efend him at the English court. Kormano succeeded not only in preserving Ben Yaʿesh’s reputation, but brought back a letter from Elizabeth to Murad in which she expressed her support for the Jewish statesman. Ben Yaʿesh’s letter o
2 CONC f thanks to Elizabeth, dated  July  28, 1592, still exists in the British archives. He sent a second envoy, Judah Ṣarfati, to London in 1593 in order to secure English neutrality in the event of war between the Ottomans and Hungar
2 CONC y; this mission too succeeded. Barton accompanied the Ottoman army on campaign and witnessed its victory at the Battle of Keresztes (1596). Barton and Ben Yaʿesh reconciled in February 1595. The Jewish diplomat sent his envoy Ṣarf
2 CONC ati to Spain on another mission in 1596 to negotiate a prisoner exchange, and two years later the Ottoman Empire concluded a peace agreement with Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In many ways, Ben Yaʿesh’s career paralleled that of his more famous predecessor and fellow Portuguese marrano Don Joseph Nasi (ca. 1524–1579), who served as a diplomat during the reign of Selim II (r. 1566– 1574). Indeed, Murad g
2 CONC ranted Ben Yaʿesh the same concession of Tiberias and its surrounding villages in Palestine that had previously been held by Don Joseph. Ben Yaʿesh sent his son Jacob (formerly Francisco) to manage its affairs, but the latter appa
2 CONC rently preferred the world of scholarship, immersing himself in religious study, to the great disappointment of his father. Still, Jacob built several houses and enjoyed popularity among the Arab population. No records indicate wh
2 CONC at happened to Jacob or the concession following the death of Ben Yaʿesh. The only reference to Ben Yaʿesh in Hebrew sources occurs on the title pages of two printings of talmudic tractates, which thank him for his support; one ca
2 CONC n surmise that Ben Yaʿesh contributed to the Jewish community in other ways as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the signing of the treaty with Spain may have marked a decline in his influence, Ben Yaʿesh remained a central figure in Ottoman politics until his death in 1603. His most lasting achievement was the establishment of an a
2 CONC lliance with England and the neutralization of Spanish forces in the eastern Mediterranean and Italy. Although he fell short of obtaining an offensive alliance between the two countries—and his efforts to send an Ottoman fleet to 
2 CONC aid England in 1588 failed because of a well-placed bribe by the Spanish ambassador—he ended the supremacy of France in Ottoman affairs, which stretched back to the time of Süleyman I Kanuni (known in Europe as Suleiman the Magnif
2 CONC icent, r. 1520–1566) and set in motion the ascendance of England as one of the dominant powers at the Porte, which continued until the end of the Ottoman Empire. By encouraging the sultans to grant the same Capitulation privileges
2 CONC  to the English as were held by the French, Ben Yaʿesh unknowingly inaugurated, as an unfortunate consequence, the Ottoman use of these privileges to try to manipulate the two powers, a practice that steadily weakened Ottoman sove
2 CONC reignty.
2 CONT
2 CONT D Gershon Lewental
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Galanté, Abraham. Don Salomon Aben Yaèche: Duc de Mételin (Istanbul: Société anonyme de Papeterie et d’Imprimerie [Fratelli Haim], 1936).
2 CONT
2 CONT ———. Histoire des Juifs de Turquie (Istanbul: Isis, 1985), vol. 1, p. 130; vol. 2, pp. 47–48; vol. 6, pp. 305–306; vol. 9, pp. 21–40.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katz, David S.“The Jewish Conspirators of Elizabethan England,” in The Jews in the History of	 England , 1485–1850 (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1996), pp. 49-106.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roth, Cecil. The House of Nasi: The Duke of	 Naxos (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1948), pp. 133–134, 205–216, 242, 247.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaw, Stanford Jay. The Jews of the	 Ottoman Empire and the	 Turkish Republic (New York: New York University Press, 1991), pp. 91–92.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolf, Lucien. “Jews in Elizabethan England,” Transactions of the Jewish Historical Society of	 England 11 (1924–27): 1–91.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation D Gershon Lewental. " Ben Yaʿesh (also Ibn Yaʿish or Abenæs), Solomon." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online , 2012. Reference. Jim Harlow. 12 December 2012 <http://
2 CONC referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopedia-of-jews-in-the-islamic-world/ben-yaesh-also-ibn-yaish-or-abens-solomon-SIM_0003730>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT salomo(n) ibn yaʿis̲h̲ (other spellings: Sallomo Abenajaex [autograph], Abenaish, Abenjaish), alias alvaro mendez (Mendes), ca. 1520-1603, a merchant and financier who was active in international and Ottoman diplomacy. He was born
2 CONC  at Tavira (Portugal) into a Marrano (‘new Christian’, crypto-Jewish) family, and made a fortune as a young man by farming the diamond mines in Narsinga (the later Madras Presidency), returning to Portugal in about 1555. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King João III made him a Knight of the Order of Santiago. He subsequently lived for various periods in Madrid, Florence and Paris. In 1585 he came to Salonica, where he openly returned to Judaism, and then settled in Istanbul. He 
2 CONC hated Spain passionately, and European diplomatic documents show that he actively and successfully fostered the development of an Anglo-Turkish entente against Spain: he had some influence at the courts of both Murād III and Queen
2 CONC  Elizabeth (whose physician Rodrigo Lopez was his brother-in-law). He also enjoyed considerable prestige in several other European courts, notably that of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His diplomatic efforts played a part in nullifying Spanish attempts to secure Ottoman neutrality in the war between England and Spain, and in ensuring England’s benevolent neutrality during the successful Ottoman campaign in Hunga
2 CONC ry which culminated in the victory at Ḥāč Ovasi̊ (Kereztes) in 1005/1596. As a reward the Sultan created him Duke of Mitylene. The grant of Tiberias, originally made to his kinsman Joseph Nasi (João Miquez, d. 1579; see nasi) was 
2 CONC renewed to him, and his own son Jacob (Francisco) settled there. Salomo ibn Yaʿīs̲h̲ died in Istanbul in 1603.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT L. Wolf, Jews in Elizabethan England, in Transactions of the Jewish Hist. Soc. of England, xi (1924-7), 1-91
2 CONT
2 CONT A. Galanté, Don Salomon Aben Yaèche, duc de Mételin, Istanbul 1936.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. A noted family called Ibn Yaʿīs̲h̲, reputedly descended from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿīs̲h̲, a 6th/12th century ¶ physician in Portugal, produced physicians, rabbis and merchants in the Ottoman Empire from the 10th/16th to the 20th centuri
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT H. Friedenwald, The Jews in medicine, Baltimore 1944, 691
2 CONT
2 CONT S. A. Rosanes, Divrei yemei Yisrael be-Togarma (i2, Tel-Aviv 1936, ii-iv (Qoroth ha-Yehudim be-Turkiyah), Sofia 1936), i2, 70, 167-8, ii, 33, iii, 77, 104, iv, 6
2 CONT
2 CONT A. Galanté, Don Salomon…, 22
2 CONT
2 CONT Jewish Enc., xii, 581-4
2 CONT
