1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Portuguese Converso; physician to Don Affonso, brother of the cardinal infante; lived in Lisbon in the sixteenth century. The shoemaker Luis Diaz, who proclaimed himself to be the Messiah, induced Francisco Mendes 
2 CONC to undergo circumcision at the age of thirty-seven and to confess Judaism (c. 1541) together with other converts. Diaz was burned at the first auto da fé held at Evora toward the end of 1542, Francisco Mendes escaping the same fat
2 CONC e by flight.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, Francisco's father, Alvaro Mendès, worked as an apprentice to a goldsmith before going to India in 1545 to manage his parents’ affairs and making his own large fortune in the diamond mines of the kingdom of Narsinga,
2 CONC  near present-day Chennai (formerly Madras).  From what scant evidence we have, Alvaro Mendes appears to have returned to Constantinople with his fortune - returning to his own family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Father of Francisco Mendes, Alvaro Mendes da Costa (Solomon Ben Yaʿish), sent his son Jacob (formerly Francisco Mendes) to manage its affairs, but the latter apparently preferred the world of scholarship, immersing himself in 
2 CONC religious study, to the great disappointment of his father. Still, Jacob built several houses and enjoyed popularity among the Arab population. No records indicate what happened to Jacob or the concession following the death of Be
2 CONC n Yaʿesh. The only reference to Ben Yaʿesh in Hebrew sources occurs on the title pages of two printings of talmudic tractates, which thank him for his support; one can surmise that Ben Yaʿesh contributed to the Jewish community in
2 CONC  other ways as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Galanté, Abraham. Don Salomon Aben Yaèche: Duc de Mételin (Istanbul: Société anonyme de Papeterie et d’Imprimerie [Fratelli Haim], 1936).
2 CONT
2 CONT ———. Histoire des Juifs de Turquie (Istanbul: Isis, 1985), vol. 1, p. 130; vol. 2, pp. 47–48; vol. 6, pp. 305–306; vol. 9, pp. 21–40.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katz, David S.“The Jewish Conspirators of Elizabethan England,” in The Jews in the History of	 England , 1485–1850 (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1996), pp. 49-106.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roth, Cecil. The House of Nasi: The Duke of	 Naxos (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1948), pp. 133–134, 205–216, 242, 247.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaw, Stanford Jay. The Jews of the	 Ottoman Empire and the	 Turkish Republic (New York: New York University Press, 1991), pp. 91–92.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolf, Lucien. “Jews in Elizabethan England,” Transactions of the Jewish Historical Society of	 England 11 (1924–27): 1–91.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation D Gershon Lewental. " Ben Yaʿesh (also Ibn Yaʿish or Abenæs), Solomon." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online , 2013. Reference. Jim Harlow. 14 January 2013 <http://r
2 CONC eferenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopedia-of-jews-in-the-islamic-world/ben-yaesh-also-ibn-yaish-or-abens-solomon-SIM_0003730>
2 CONT
2 CONT Informatione Sommaria, reprinted in Grätz, Gesch. 3d ed., ix. 551; Historia da Inquisição, p. 9, Lisbon, 1845.
2 CONT
