1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Peter Leaman 
2 CONT *Born 1680 in Bern, Switzerland
2 CONT * Died 11 Jun 1741 at about age 61 in Lampeter, Lancaster, Pennsylvania
2 CONT * Son of Peter Lehman and Barbara (Jung) Lehman
2 CONT *Brother of Maria Lehman and Hans Lehman
2 CONT * Husband of Ann (Unknown) Leman — married [date unknown] 
2 CONT * Husband of Anna Maria (Willems) Lehman — married about 1700 [location unknown]
2 CONT * Father of Peter Leman, Barbara (Lehman) Leman, Jacob Leman, Abraham Leman, Christian Leman, Daniel Leman, Elizabeth Leman, Henry Leman, Issac Leman, John Leman and Mary Leman
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=35906496 Find a Grave]
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter spelled his last name Leman in his will dated April 29, 1741, however it is spelled Leaman in a number of other sources. According to a History of Lancaster County, this family of Lemans were Huguenots and entirely distinct 
2 CONC from the family of Lehman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Ann LEAMAN. Peter LEAMAN Sr. and Ann LEAMAN were married. Peter married Anna in Switzerland. They arrived in Philadelphia in 1717 and were early settlers in Lampeter Township, Lancaster, County. Peter Leman was among the f
2 CONC irst pioneers to receive title to land in East Lampeter Township through a syndicate of London merchants authorized by William Penn. He first purchased 300 acres in 1717 and then added an adjacent plot of 400 acres in 1733 which p
2 CONC robably was swamp land at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lehman-1475
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was born in 1680, in the Emmental region Canton Bern, Switzerland. He was raised in a strict Mennonite family, who eventually fled the area due to religious persecution. The family migrated west to eastern Germany. From Germ
2 CONC any, Peter continued on to England, where he boarded a ship bound for the New World.
2 CONT
2 CONT In either late summer or early fall of 1717, Peter Lehman/Leman arrived in Philadelphia on a ship as part of a three-ship convoy-widowed-and with "five motherless children" (Peter, Christian, Jacob, Barbara, and Mary) in tow (The 
2 CONC other children, born to him through his second wife, Ann were Henry, John, Abraham, Isaac, Daniel and Elizabeth). His first wife, Anna, seems to have died shortly after the birth of her youngest child in about 1707.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his arrival, Peter is mentioned in a warrant dated 22 Nov 1717. It states that there was to be deeded 300 acres to him in Lancaster County. "These foreigners were informed that they should be naturalized if they expe
2 CONC ct(ed) their children to be able to inherit the land" (Layman, 14-15). This same 300 acres was to also have had a Layman family cemetery within its bounds-which has since been cultivated over (Peter's grave was included in the fam
2 CONC ily plot).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT <[http://lehmanfamilyhistory.com/Category_A.html Lehman, Laymanf, Lemon Genealogy - DNA Enhanced.  Category A]>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT FamilyA.1. The Most Numerous Lehman, Layman, Lemon, etc. line 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Leman arrived in Philadelphia in August of 1717 with five motherless children. On dates unknown, but proximate in time to Peter's arrival; two close relatives, Rudolph and Hans, first set foot on the shores of the New World,
2 CONC  almost surely at Philadelphia. A fourth relative may have immigrated later, but this is speculation at this point. The family is the most prolific in the DNA study. The precise proportion changes, of course, with each result but 
2 CONC this family accounts for about one third of the U. S. participants. Multiple descendants of all three  have been tested. Several participants who exhibit this genetic signature have not yet been able to trace their line to the imm
2 CONC igrant.  Cousins in Switzerland trace the family to Vechigen at the beginning of Kirchebuch records in the mid-1500s, as appears from Test 68, below. All of the three known  early immigrants were Mennonites. We will call it Family
2 CONC A.1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family A.1 and Family A.2, as well as several other families in the study, are predominately, and herein except as noted otherwise, are of  Haplogroup R-M269. Haplogroup R-M269 is the dominant lineage in all of Western Europe toda
2 CONC y. It is found in low frequencies in Turkey and the northern Fertile Crescent, while its highest frequencies are in Western Europe. Yhis and all other Haplogroup information on this web site is taken from FTDNA's web site.
2 CONT
2 CONT The charts that follow include, as to this and all other families, only direct ancestors of test participants. Other descendants of the immigrant may be found in Lehman, Layman Genealogy Handbook and its 2011 Supplement and 2014 U
2 CONC pdate.
2 CONT
