1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Her killers, likely the Tanos of Galisteo during the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 took her two daughters captive. They were found by Juan Olguin at San Juan. The older sister was the Angela de Leyva who married Cristobal 
2 CONC Torres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana married José de Leyva de Nevares, son of Pedro de Leyva and Catalina García Holgado, about 1668. (José de Leyva de Nevares was born in 1649 in Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España and died after 1693.)
